 ```
 
+##### onRetry(context)
+
+Type: `Function`
+
+Callback invoked only when another attempt is actually going to happen, right before the wait. Receives the same context object as `onFailedAttempt`. Unlike `onFailedAttempt`, it is not called for the final failure, which makes it suited to retry metrics.
+
+The function is called _after_ `onFailedAttempt`, `shouldConsumeRetry`, and `shouldRetry`. It is also called when `shouldConsumeRetry` returns `false`, since another attempt follows then too.
+
+The function is _not_ called on `AbortError`, on an abort through `signal`, on `TypeError` (except network errors), if `retries` or `maxRetryTime` are exhausted, or if `shouldRetry` returns `false`.
+
+The function can return a promise. If it throws or rejects, all retries will be aborted and the original promise will reject with the thrown error.
+
+```js
+import pRetry from 'p-retry';
+
+let retryCount = 0;
+
+const result = await pRetry(run, {
+	onRetry: () => {
+		retryCount++;
+	}
+});
+```
+
 ##### shouldRetry(context)
 
 Type: `Function`
@@ -224,6 +248,15 @@ Measured with a monotonic clock (`performance.now()`) so system clock adjustment
 
 The limit is checked after failed attempts and callbacks, before scheduling a retry. The retry delay is shortened to the remaining time, but the scheduled retry can still start after the limit. Attempts and callbacks are never interrupted, so they can run past the limit.
 
+##### initialDelay
+
+Type: `number`\
+Default: `0`
+
+The number of milliseconds to wait before the first attempt. With the default of `0`, the first attempt runs immediately.
+
+If `signal` aborts during the wait, the promise rejects with the abort reason and `input` is never called. The wait is capped at 2147483647ms like other delays, and it does not count towards `maxRetryTime`, which starts measuring when the wait ends.
+
 ##### signal
 
 Type: [`AbortSignal`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/AbortSignal)
